# Sources for the small Front delegation and wider oil coalition: Ervand
# Abrahamian, Iran Between Two Revolutions, ch. 5, pp. 250–267;
# Painter and Brew, The Struggle for Iran, ch. 1.
# The recurring outreach and its effects are counterfactual.
title: Deputy Outreach
new-page: true
is-card: true
tags: parliamentary_affairs
view-if: deputy_outreach_timer <= 0 and majles_convened = 1 and nationalization_approved_majles = 0
on-arrival: month_actions += 1; deputy_outreach_timer = 3

= Deputy Outreach

Many deputies who are not part of the
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} may still support a particular
constitutional motion or oil position. Outreach works best when it asks for a
specific commitment rather than treating every potential vote as a new member
of the coalition.

Choose the case {!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!}
deputies will carry into private conversations this
month.

- @deputies_procedure
- @deputies_oil
- @deputies_constituency
- @return_card

@deputies_procedure
title: Emphasize parliamentary procedure
subtitle: Deputies outside the <span class="term term-national-front">Front</span> are asked to defend rules before committing to its program
on-arrival: {!
Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy +=
    Q.parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy < 70 ? 4 : 1;
Q.constitutional_legitimacy += Q.constitutional_legitimacy < 70 ? 2 : 1;
!}

{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} deputies ask
colleagues to defend the chamber's own procedures even
where they disagree with the coalition's broader program. The appeal offers a
limited commitment that constitutional conservatives can support.

Procedural credibility grows across factional lines. No one mistakes the
conversation for endorsement of nationalization or
{!<span class="term term-national-front">!}Front{!</span>!} membership.
[? if parliamentary_procedure_legitimacy >= 70: The deputies most receptive
to a procedural appeal have heard it before. Further conversations hold that
ground, but produce few new commitments without a substantive agreement. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@deputies_oil
title: Ask for support for nationalization
subtitle: A direct pledge separates allies on nationalization from allies on everything else
on-arrival: {!
Q.oil_coalition_support += Q.oil_coalition_support < 70 ? 5 : 2;
Q.public_mandate += Q.public_mandate < 70 ? 2 : 0;
if (Q.oil_coalition_support >= 80) Q.independent_nationalists_dissent += 1;
!}

The outreach makes the nationalization principle the explicit question and
asks deputies where they stand. Supporters receive a common explanation they
can defend to colleagues and constituents.

The potential oil majority becomes firmer and more visible. Deputies who might
have supported only a narrower procedural motion are harder to bring along.
[? if oil_coalition_support >= 75: The remaining holdouts are not waiting for
another statement of principle. Persuading them will require answers on
procedure, attendance, or the terms they still dispute. ?]

- @post_event: Continue

@deputies_constituency
title: Share constituency-pressure reports
subtitle: Reports from home show undecided deputies that oil is no longer only a Tehran argument
on-arrival: {!
Q.organizational_reach += Q.organizational_reach < 70 ? 2 : 0;
Q.oil_coalition_support += Q.oil_coalition_support < 70 ? 4 : 1;
!}

Organizers summarize verified local reports for deputies without claiming a
nationwide plebiscite. The material shows where oil has become an urgent
constituency question and where the coalition still lacks evidence.

Deputies gain a clearer sense of pressure outside Tehran, while the
secretariat improves the channel by which local information reaches the
chamber.
[? if organizational_reach >= 70: The same well-organized constituencies are
now supplying most of the letters and delegations. Their pressure remains
real, but repetition cannot make it appear nationwide. ?]

- @post_event: Continue
